WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.240
Can a stranger send email that
looks like it comes from our CFO?

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.230
Email breaks in four ways.

00:00:07.583 --> 00:00:09.253
First, someone listens in.

00:00:09.523 --> 00:00:12.433
A message crosses several
networks before it lands.

00:00:12.813 --> 00:00:15.783
If one hop uses plaintext instead of TLS,

00:00:16.253 --> 00:00:18.003
whoever sits there can read it.

00:00:18.183 --> 00:00:20.293
Nothing breaks and no alert fires,

00:00:20.743 --> 00:00:22.553
so it can go unnoticed for years.

00:00:23.683 --> 00:00:25.973
Second, someone rewrites the message.

00:00:26.263 --> 00:00:30.943
Often the attacker writes from a hacked
supplier mailbox and changes one bank

00:00:30.943 --> 00:00:31.893
account number.

00:00:32.118 --> 00:00:36.448
Both sides see a normal thread,
and the mismatch surfaces weeks later.

00:00:36.698 --> 00:00:38.028
Consider a call-back:

00:00:38.458 --> 00:00:42.048
confirm any bank change by phone,
on a number you already have.

00:00:43.083 --> 00:00:45.033
Third, someone pretends to be us.

00:00:45.348 --> 00:00:50.308
Nothing in the email protocol stops a
stranger from typing our domain into the

00:00:50.308 --> 00:00:51.018
From field.

00:00:51.353 --> 00:00:53.303
The cost is CEO fraud,

00:00:53.693 --> 00:00:57.983
phishing of our customers under our brand,
and our own mail landing in spam.

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:01.080
Fourth, someone sits in the middle.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:04.450
Encryption between mail
servers is optional by default,

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:09.690
so an attacker can strip the offer,
and both servers fall back to plaintext.

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:13.280
From there, they read everything
and can change anything.

00:01:13.555 --> 00:01:15.365
That is why it matters at the top.

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:18.330
It defeats the assumption
that we have encryption.

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:20.760
What good looks like.

00:01:21.045 --> 00:01:23.775
SPF lists who may send as our domain.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:26.010
DKIM signs each message.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:29.570
DMARC tells receivers what
to do when those fail.

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:31.060
Only reject blocks.

00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:34.580
MTA-STS and DANE make
encryption mandatory,

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:36.680
so a stripped connection fails.

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:40.170
These are DNS records
and mail server settings,

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.390
so the cost is engineering hours.

00:01:42.875 --> 00:01:46.765
NIS2 Article 21 lists cyber
hygiene and encryption,

00:01:47.255 --> 00:01:49.205
and these controls help show both.

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:52.240
A suggested first step.

00:01:52.525 --> 00:01:57.115
Ask whether we move DMARC to reject,
and MTA-STS to enforce.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:01.050
The monitoring modes cost the
same effort and block nothing.

00:02:01.315 --> 00:02:03.725
A free online test shows where we stand.

00:02:05.283 --> 00:02:06.813
Four ways email breaks.

00:02:07.188 --> 00:02:10.278
Enforce the records,
and keep the call-back for payments.

